questions.prompt:
**(Design + analysis, cumulative)** Given a `vector<int> v` of N distinct ints and an integer 1 <= k < N, return the **k smallest values in sorted order**. Give (a) a design based on quickselect (use the STL function that implements it), (b) a design based on a heap, and (c) the simple sort-everything design. Write the function for (a) in C++, and give the time complexity of all three, as a function of N and k, with the case k = sqrt(N) worked out.

```cpp
vector<int> kSmallest(vector<int> v, int k) {
    // write code here
}
```

questions.answer:
**(a) Quickselect design.** `std::nth_element` partitions so that the element at position k is the one that would be there in sorted order, everything before it is <= it (unordered). Then sort only the first k.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

vector<int> kSmallest(vector<int> v, int k) {
    nth_element(v.begin(), v.begin() + k, v.end());   // average O(N), worst O(N) for introselect, O(N^2) for plain quickselect
    v.resize(k);                                       // keep the k smallest (unsorted)
    sort(v.begin(), v.end());                          // O(k log k)
    return v;
}
```
Time: O(N) average for nth_element (quickselect) + O(k log k) = **O(N + k log k) average**. Copying the vector is O(N).

**(b) Heap design.** `make_heap(v.begin(), v.end(), greater<int>{})` makes a min-heap in O(N), then k `pop_heap` calls, each O(log N), appending the popped values: **O(N + k log N)**, and the output comes out already sorted. (Alternative: max-heap of size k scanning N elements, O(N log k), then sort/pop the k: O(N log k + k log k).)

**(c) Sort everything:** `sort(v)`, take the first k: **O(N log N)**.

**k = sqrt(N):** (a) O(N + sqrt N log sqrt N) = **O(N)**; (b) O(N + sqrt N log N) = **O(N)**; (size-k heap: O(N log sqrt N) = O(N log N)); (c) O(N log N). For k = N/2: all three are O(N log N) except that quickselect+sort of the half is O(N + (N/2) log(N/2)) = O(N log N) as well. So (a) or (b) win when k is small, and they differ only in average vs guaranteed behaviour.
